WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:06.790
This FIFA executive used an Onion article as proof&nbsp;
to defend himself against corruption charges. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:11.350
the uninitiated, The Onion is a satirical&nbsp;
newspaper. This is Jack Warner. He was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:16.510
vice president of FIFA. And in 2015, he was one&nbsp;
of several FIFA officials charged with fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.470
racketeering, and money laundering. I know&nbsp;
this because I made a giant video about FIFA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.070
corruption and know way too much about this&nbsp;
organization. But I had to cut this tidbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.150
because it just didn't fit. But this one's just&nbsp;
too good not to share. Warner recorded a video of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:33.910
himself holding up this obviously fake article,&nbsp;
which says that FIFA had announced an impromptu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:39.490
2015 World Cup that was starting that very&nbsp;
day. He thought it was a genuine news report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:44.770
and he held it up as evidence that there was an&nbsp;
American conspiracy to get back at FIFA for not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.850
choosing them to host the World Cup. I mean,&nbsp;
just a skim through this article reveals how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:54.790
clearly satirical it is. It ends by saying the&nbsp;
U.S. was leading the defending champs, Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:59.830
in the opening match, getting 12 penalties&nbsp;
in the first 3 minutes. But even still

